\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Ideal labels of a first-order calculus}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{X} be a finite set and let #{k} be a commutative unital
ring. Write #{A} for the ring of functions #{X\to k}, with
pointwise addition and multiplication. For #{x\neq y} in #{X},
let #{e_{xy}} be a symbol, and let #{\Omega} be the direct sum
of one copy of #{k} for each such pair. A function #{f\in A}
acts on the left and on the right by
##{(f\cdot a)_{xy}=f(x)a_{xy},\qquad
 (a\cdot f)_{xy}=a_{xy}f(y).}
An #{A}-sub-bimodule of #{\Omega} is an additive subgroup closed
under both actions. These sub-bimodules are exactly the sums
##{N=\bigoplus_{x\neq y}I_{xy}e_{xy},}
where each #{I_{xy}} is an ideal of #{k}. The quotient bimodule
has coefficient ring #{k/I_{xy}} on the pair #{(x,y)}. When
#{k} is a field, each ideal is #{0} or #{k}, so the quotient is
the free calculus on a directed graph with no loop. That field
case is the correspondence of
\citet{pp. 3159-3178}{dimakis1994finite}.}

\proof{
\p{Let #{\delta_z} be the function with value #{1} at #{z} and
value #{0} elsewhere. This small delta is the Kronecker delta,
including #{\delta_x(y)=\delta_{xy}}. It corresponds to the Dirac
delta at one point and to the position eigenvector there. The
codifferential of continuum forms is the adjoint #{\extdag} of
\ref{fdip-00PV}. For #{v\in N} and #{x\neq y},
##{\delta_x\cdot v\cdot\delta_y=v_{xy}e_{xy}}
lies in #{N}, because #{N} is a sub-bimodule. Thus every
component of an element of #{N} lies in #{N}. Let
#{I_{xy}} be the set of #{c\in k} for which #{c e_{xy}} lies in
#{N}. If #{c} and #{d} lie in #{I_{xy}}, then so does #{c+d}.
If #{r\in k} and #{c\in I_{xy}}, the constant function of value
#{r} multiplies #{c e_{xy}} to #{(rc)e_{xy}}, so #{rc} lies in
#{I_{xy}}. Hence #{I_{xy}} is an ideal, and
#{N} is the displayed sum.}

\p{Conversely, fix an ideal #{I_{xy}} for each ordered pair
#{x\neq y}. The displayed sum is an additive subgroup. Left
multiplication by #{f} multiplies the #{(x,y)} component by
#{f(x)}, and right multiplication multiplies it by #{f(y)}.
Both scalars preserve the ideal, so the sum is a sub-bimodule.
The quotient on that component is #{k/I_{xy}}.}

\p{A field has exactly two ideals, #{0} and itself. Keeping the
pairs with ideal #{0} and discarding the pairs with ideal
#{k} is a directed graph with no loop. For a ring that is not a
field the same description is too small. Take #{k=\mathbb Z}
and one ordered pair with ideal #{2\mathbb Z}, and take every
other ideal to be #{0}. The quotient coefficient on that pair
is #{\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z}, which is neither #{0} nor
#{\mathbb Z}.}
}
